WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:09.990
Malcolm Grant: May we move on then to the
governance part of the agenda, for which the

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:16.539
first item is Tim's paper on our relationship
with the platform for Social Care Information

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:17.070
Centre.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:22.429
Tim Kelsey: Thank you Malcolm. So if the Board
remembers, at its last meeting it asked for

00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:27.859
an assurance really that the relationship
with the Informational Centre, given the very

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:34.570
critical importance of that relationship,
was working well and where there were challenges.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.770
So this paper tries to outline those issues.
If I can just give a quick overview of that.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:46.530
Clearly for us there is a recognition that
data and digital technology is fundamental

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.660
to everything we are trying to achieve, from
prevention through new models of care, to

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:58.660
sustainability, and that we need to embed
those enablers as quickly as possible in our

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:02.149
health and care services.

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:07.330
The Information Centre has a unique statutory
role in helping us deliver that objective.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:12.540
In the first instance, it is the only body
under the Health and Social Care Act that

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.340
is allowed to handle sensitive, potentially
identifiable data and, as a result of which,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.390
has both an extraordinary opportunity, but
also an obligation to be a world class data

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.490
centre powering all the commissioning requirements
we have, but also much more broadly, the kind

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:34.490
of research opportunity that also exists in
properly exploiting the data assets of the

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.909
NHS for the best benefits of patients and
citizens at large.

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:46.909
Besides, its unique statutory position as
the Data Centre, it is also a centre of excellence

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.979
in the programme management of technology
programmes. And many of our key programmes,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:58.860
those of which we have collective SRO[?] responsibility,
for example, are almost entirely commissioned

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:04.829
from the information centre. So for these
two core reasons, the relationship with the

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:06.619
Information Centre is critical.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:12.090
The Board, in a formal sense, is asked to
agree to a Memorandum of Understanding, which

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:19.090
is contained here, which tries to provide
a basis on which we can have a relationship

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.419
of transparency and accountability between
both us and them. On the one hand, we need

00:02:24.419 --> 00:02:30.070
to be a good customer and they need to be
a good supporting partner. That is what the

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:36.160
MU is there to do, to describe some standards
against we can both judge our success, in

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.850
holding ourselves accountable for a proper
collaborative partnership.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:43.769
However, if I could just bring the Board's
attention, in that context, to five core themes,

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:49.639
which people may want debate and interrogate.
The first issue, which is a very immediate

00:02:49.639 --> 00:02:54.820
one, is how we collectively resolve the data
problems that face commissioners in particular,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:01.320
but others of our system here today. There
are significant gaps in our insight into services

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:07.410
that are commissioned. For example, as people
will know - and I will come to this in a second

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.260
- we are not currently able to routinely link
primary and secondary care data to enable

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.560
us, for example, to analyse the experience
and outcome of the patient across the pathway

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.169
of care. So a very urgent requirement, which
Paul may want to speak to, is that we solve

00:03:22.169 --> 00:03:28.290
that problem. We have called that problem
the Operational Information for Commissioners

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.639
programme, which I will come to in a second.

00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:35.449
The second key challenge, which again is an
immediate one, which is very complex, which

00:03:35.449 --> 00:03:40.090
Karen is leading, is how we collaboratively
resolve the information governance issues

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:47.090
that impede easy, appropriate access to sometimes
sensitive data by doctors and clinicians and

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:53.250
others on the frontline, as well as commissioners.
The Health and Social Care Act frankly did

00:03:53.250 --> 00:04:00.190
us no great favours in some regards, in relation
to it is the legal position around these issues.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.889
However, again, I hope the Board will be reassured
that actually the work Karen is doing with

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:07.889
Andy Williams, the new chief executive for
the Information Centre, is moving the dial

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:09.259
quite rapidly on coming to a new place.

00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:09.419
[BREAK IN ADUIO]

00:04:09.419 --> 00:04:15.430
Karen Wheeler: - by the HSCIC, the Information
Centre and enable connection of national database

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:22.430
and local database, which is [inaudible] patient
need[?] to be able to access the data that

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:47.380
they need for the commissioning, but in a
secure way. At the moment that is the [inaudible].

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:10.690
It will take two years, and there is a lot
of work to get there. However, I think we

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:18.730
have an end state that we can move towards
much more quickly. Our relationship with the

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:22.940
Information Centre, is obviously, absolutely
critical on that. They have been very helpful

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.010
in the work that we are been doing.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:30.250
Paul Baumann: I think we spoke in the Board
before about the Operational Information for

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.400
Commissioners programme, so I will not reintroduce
all of that. We have got urgent work underway

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.400
to rectify what strikes me as being a fissure
that there has always been in the information

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.250
flow in the NHS and which has not got better
through the reform, but neither has it been

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:48.840
invented by the reforms, which really is being
able to link together financial information,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.840
the activity trends that we talk about from
time to time, the performance metrics that

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.830
flow off this and then in the complete package,
as it were, the outcomes which flow at the

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:02.550
end of that. We have got a very, very urgent
programme of work to try and get first two

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:08.210
of those linked up because it is just so important
as we go through the challenging months and

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:12.090
years that we have got ahead of us. We have
got a longer term programme to build what

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:18.800
I hope will be a way of looking at the information
for commissioning, which brings together those

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:24.030
things, rather than seeing them as individual
components to be discussed. However, another

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:31.030
time would be the best time, I think, to talk
through the detail of all of that.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:36.010
Tim Kelsey: Okay, so in the medium term, the
relationship, the core focus for the information

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:39.940
centre is to do what Paul in way just described,
not just for commissioners, but to develop

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.750
a data strategy that will specify for the
Health and Care system, what the Information

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.910
Centre needs to deliver to bring together
a variety of data assets on outside, currently

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:56.530
Information Centre, others within it, in a
much more meaningful way, so that we can start

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:02.710
properly extracting both analytic value but
also value for direct care provision as well,

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:09.340
from these data services. A key component
there is care.data. I do not know whether

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.440
you would like me to give you an update now
on care.data.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.250
Malcom Grant: I think we would appreciate
it at this meeting.

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:20.650
Tim Kelsey: So this has been a programme which
we worked very, very closely on with the Information

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:25.210
Centre. In fact, the team that is delivering
care.data, is a joint team of NHS England

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:32.210
and Information Centre personnel. The current
status of the project is that, as the Board

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.530
will remember back in February, we brought
it to a halt because of widespread and I think

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:41.080
legitimate concerns about the way in which
we were able to describe both the narrative

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:44.610
of benefits for that programme, but perhaps,
more importantly, the way in which we were

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:50.000
able to safeguard or provide a proper level
of safeguard for people's data when they chose

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.510
to enable us to analyse it.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:59.440
Where we are today is that an enormous amount
of work has taken place with six CCGs in four

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:06.330
regions to, frankly, redesign the entire programme,
in a very co-created way, with local clinicians

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:13.330
and the local communities. We are now at a
point where Dame Fiona Caldicott, whom the

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:20.470
Secretary of State has invited to provide
him with assurance that this programme is

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:27.470
in a proper place, has now published the criteria
against which she will judge its success in

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:36.899
meeting that standard. We are, very shortly,
to officially reply to that report. I hope

00:08:36.899 --> 00:08:43.000
Dame Fiona will then give the Secretary of
State advice that we can proceed to the pathfinder

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:48.690
phase, which is to investigate whether these
new approaches are, indeed, going to deliver.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.380
At the end of the pathfinder phase, which
probably will last for three or four months,

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:58.470
Dame Fiona will then take a view and advise
accordingly on what the next steps will be.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:04.500
So I would actually really just like to take
the opportunity of thanking colleagues in

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:10.500
CCGs, particularly the pathfinder CCGs, who
have, with real diligence, been involved.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:17.500
If I may, I was at the Health Select Committee,
updating members on progress with the programme

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:23.300
last week. I read to them a quotation from
Sarah Schofield, who is the chairman of West

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:27.790
Hampshire CCG, just outlining why she feels
this is such an important programme. If I

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:33.250
may, just to repeat it, 'Whilst the sharing
of patient information across the NHS helps

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:39.080
to ensure that the quality and safety of services
is consistent and provides valuable insight

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:43.650
on diseases and conditions, it is essential
that this is done is an appropriate and secure

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:48.100
way. We are therefore extremely pleased to
have the opportunity to work with our patients,

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:53.230
to test communications on care.data and ensure
that GPs are equipped to manage this locally

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:57.640
and are able to support patients with making
informed choices.' I think that genuinely

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:04.290
reflects a view that is taken widely across
the pathfinder community.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:08.480
So that is where we are at with care.data
and people may have questions afterwards.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.870
If I can just quickly move onto two other
core areas, which are outlined in the document,

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:17.390
which I will just cover very briefly. The
first of those, as I say, are our collaboration

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:22.010
around delivery of digital and technology
programmes, amongst which are most of the

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:27.770
ones that we have described, NHS Choices,
and so on. I think we are in a very good place

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:32.690
now with the Information Centre, in relation
to those programmes. There are lots of issues

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:36.180
which we need to resolve collectively, as
a system, around ongoing governance to make

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:41.640
that the programmes are absolutely meeting
MPA, major projects authority, Cabinet Office

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:46.540
requirements. However, increasing I think
this collaboration is delivering real benefit

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.450
and a much more professional, if I can put
it like that way, approach to delivery of

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:56.930
what, in the past, have been complex and often
perhaps sometimes programmes that have not

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:01.830
always delivered all the benefits which they
were funded.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:07.080
Finally, the Information Centre and ourselves,
along with the Department of Health and other

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:12.060
system leaders, are collaborators in the National
Information Board framework and the paper

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:18.149
here just outlines, a little bit, the governance
that is now in place around broadly data and

00:11:18.149 --> 00:11:24.420
digital technology, nationally commissioned.
I chair the National Information Board as

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.529
Information Director and it is proving, I
think, to be a very important vehicle for

00:11:28.529 --> 00:11:34.210
the system to achieve consensus around a strategic
framework for action. And that was published

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:38.710
last November. However, we are moving very
rapidly now to implementation. I described

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:43.750
in the document the three core programmes
of implementation that we, NHS England, are

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.800
collaborating on, specifically.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.690
I would like to commend frankly the relationship
we have with the Information Centre, to the

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:53.649
Board. There is lots of work we have to do
urgently and not a little bit of challenge

00:11:53.649 --> 00:11:57.890
in delivering these outputs. However, Andy
Williams and Kingsley Manning, the chairmen,

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:04.330
have put an awful lot of time into the collaboration.
I hope the Board will agree to the signing

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.050
of the MU.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:11.350
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much Tim. Let
me invite questions and comments to Tim.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:19.460
Noel Gordon: Tim, I think the MU, Tim, is
an important and significant step forward

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:26.520
because it seems to embody a lot of our learnings
from past, big technology programmes, but

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:33.520
also a lot of external best practice. Quite
rightly, it puts a lot of responsibility on

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:40.730
us to define our requirements very clearly
for the delivery organisations. Where do you

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:49.000
think we are, are we in a good place in having
built consensus internally and what are our

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.500
requirements are going to be for the next
two to three years? Maybe even talk a little

00:12:53.500 --> 00:13:00.500
bit about the role of the NIB in trying to
stabilise those requirements so HSCIC has

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.430
something coherent to work from.

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:07.630
Tim Kelsey: So, actually, I did not mention
but one of the asks in this paper is for the

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:12.570
Board to confirm the assumption of a Data
Board, precisely to meet their requirements.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:18.089
So when we discuss the Information Centre,
they are sitting at a similar, receiving unit

00:13:18.089 --> 00:13:21.920
at their end, that we are absolutely clear
and consistent about what are our specifications

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.649
and as far ahead of time as possible.

00:13:23.649 --> 00:13:27.430
Noel Gordon: I am thinking by the way of not
just data but also delivery[?] technologies,

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:28.000
too.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.580
Tim Kelsey: I understand. So to some extent,
on the data side, if I may for a minute, we

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:38.120
have an obligation as a commissioner, to be
very clear on behalf of local commissioners

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:42.360
as well as ourselves on what the requirement
is. So partly that comes out of Paul's work,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:47.420
but supported by work that the Chief Data
Officer, Geraint Lewis, is doing. We aim to

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:51.580
be able to be very coherent about an organisation
about what that requirement is.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:56.990
However, much more broadly, of course, that
is just one voice across an entire health

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:03.930
and care system. The importance of the National
Information Board's position is that one of

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:10.300
the commitments it has made is to come up
with a very clear data strategy, on the data

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:17.300
side again, by June, across the system, piece
of works being shared between CQC and NHS

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:25.560
England, of which our voice is one feed. So
we have got this very significant challenge

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:30.000
in trying to be articulate about such an enormous
strategic requirement. However, we have to

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.410
do it or we are not going to be able to properly
specify for the Information Centres to turn

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.100
into the service we need.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.990
So that perhaps helps clarify on the data
side, but there is an enormous amount of work

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:48.990
going on there. On the broader, digital technology,
mobile issues, again, the whole work of the

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.730
National Information Board is to recognise
that none of those questions can be answered

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:00.310
by individual, arms' length bodies. The only
way we can answer those questions and make

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:04.500
sensible investment decisions in the future,
and even make sensible representations to

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:08.760
the next spending review, are if we speak
as one voice across the system. I think we

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:14.350
are getting there with that. I hope that by
the time the National Information Board begins

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:21.350
to agree some of the actions of implementation,
people will see a real commitment to collaboration

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:23.130
and getting to the right answers.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:30.130
Moira Gibb: Malcolm, I should have declared
another interest which is as a non-executive

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:37.490
director of the UK Statistics Authority. Obviously
HSCIC is also a producer of statistics and

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:48.680
there is code of practice by which they will
be judged. It would be good to see some commitment

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:56.230
to that aspect of the work in the memorandum
of understanding because obviously the code

00:15:56.579 --> 00:16:02.070
of practice starts from the position of users,
which is much wider, obviously, than the NHS

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:07.180
system. The protection that the National Statistics
label gives to the public's trust, as it were.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:14.180
Tim Kelsey: Yeah, that is a very good point
and I will take that.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:15.680
Moira Gibb: Thank you.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:19.959
Malcolm Grant: Right. Thank you very much,
Tim, for that update, which is very comprehensive

00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:25.610
as [inaudible]. I am sure that you will be
planning to come back to us shortly with further

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.870
detail on care.data in particular, as it develops.

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:29.870
Tim Kelsey: Yes.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:34.740
Malcolm Grant: Good. Thank you very much.
May I then move the Board on to Item T10,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:41.200
which is the Transformation of Primary Care
Support Services and get Karen to speak to

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:41.560
it.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:47.410
Karen Wheeler: Yes, very happy to. So the
purpose of this paper is to bring you some

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:52.360
assurance of the progress that we are making
on the programme to transform primary care

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:59.360
support services. So let me just run through
some of the key progress we have been making.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:07.950
Firstly, I just to remind people of why we
are doing this, the purpose of transforming

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:15.250
this key service area, is to ensure that we
are able to continue to deliver high quality

00:17:15.769 --> 00:17:22.079
services to our primary care stakeholders,
and improved services and enable those to

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:29.079
be modernised within an affordable cost envelope.
So this is an area that did not get properly

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:38.179
transformed and modernised at the point that
NHS England was implemented in the rest of

00:17:38.179 --> 00:17:45.179
the reforms. So it is to that extent, overdue.
I am very conscious that the staff in primary

00:17:46.159 --> 00:17:51.960
care support services have been waiting for
this transformation for a long time. They

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:58.609
are frustrated it is taken us so long to get
to the transformation and the change. I do

00:17:58.609 --> 00:18:05.609
want to reflect the difficulty that this has
created for staff who know that change is

00:18:06.559 --> 00:18:08.739
coming.

00:18:08.739 --> 00:18:15.739
Since we have set up the programme, I think
we have moved quickly on the process to put

00:18:17.269 --> 00:18:23.200
in place a procurement for a supplier to take
over the service, as that was the decision

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:30.200
that the Board made last year. We launched
the procurement at the beginning of November.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:38.710
We are at the point where we have identified
three shortlist bidders and we are working

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:45.710
with those bidders on their bids. Our current
aim is to finalise those bids in February,

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:58.350
so that we can then complete our business
case and make a decision for the new service.

00:18:59.119 --> 00:19:06.119
Bear in mind, we are now where we are in the
calendar year and in the electoral cycle,

00:19:08.399 --> 00:19:15.399
we will not be expecting to make an award
of contract to our original timetable, which

00:19:16.389 --> 00:19:23.389
was the end of March. It will now be after
May sometime. We are working through the implications

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:33.980
for that change of timetable because that
will have implications for not only the transformation

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:42.180
of the service, but also the cost implications,
and we are working through that as we speak.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:49.340
Bearing in mind that does mean the programme
is going to extend, we have upped the pace

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:57.380
on the work that we need to do, to make efficiencies
and stabilise the service as it is. We have

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:04.309
put the service under a National Management
Structure, which reports into me. As you know

00:20:04.309 --> 00:20:13.149
before, it was under the four regions. I think
that does help to stabilise it and it certainly

00:20:13.289 --> 00:20:16.389
will help to manage the transition.

00:20:16.389 --> 00:20:21.529
We can see lots of opportunities for efficiencies
that we can make even before we move this

00:20:21.529 --> 00:20:27.919
service to a new provider and have started
to do that, which will help us both make savings

00:20:27.919 --> 00:20:34.919
but also transform and transfer the service
safely to the new provider, when that decision

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:40.619
is made.
We are trying to work on both of these fronts,

00:20:40.619 --> 00:20:47.619
both on the procurement front and the interim
efficiencies, very closely with staff and

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:56.259
our stakeholders. Just this week, we had two
big events with staff and with stakeholders,

00:20:56.259 --> 00:21:03.259
who both met the shortlisted suppliers as
well. I believe those events went very successfully

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:11.429
and they were able to expand on the approach
that they are planning to bring to the transformation.

00:21:11.559 --> 00:21:18.389
I think that was very reassuring, although
obviously staff have a lot of concerns still,

00:21:18.389 --> 00:21:25.389
as they are understandably concerned about
the impacts on them and their jobs.

00:21:25.549 --> 00:21:32.379
So that is probably the key things that I
wanted to say right now. As I said, I just

00:21:32.379 --> 00:21:38.359
wanted to emphasise my thanks to the staff
and indeed the trade union representatives

00:21:38.359 --> 00:21:41.789
of the staff, who we are working very constructively
on this programme.

00:21:41.789 --> 00:21:46.809
Malcolm Grant: Thank you Karen. May I invite
some questions and comments from the Board?

00:21:46.809 --> 00:21:53.809
No? In that case, can we just re-emphasise
what you just said? This is a really complex

00:21:54.289 --> 00:22:01.289
issue on which pace and process are our intention.
I think it is been prolonged for far further

00:22:02.749 --> 00:22:07.899
than we and the staff would have wished, but
for good reason, as it happens, at each point

00:22:07.899 --> 00:22:14.899
and both reviewed those in a public session
and the Board on each occasion. We also appreciate

00:22:15.299 --> 00:22:21.619
the real challenge of it showing continuity
of high quality service during a period of

00:22:21.619 --> 00:22:27.389
instability and particularly for staff. I
was quite taken with your observations about

00:22:27.389 --> 00:22:34.389
staff and stakeholder engagement and I hope
that signals our ability to deliver this with

00:22:34.499 --> 00:22:41.499
the dignity, as well as the outcome, that
we would wish. Thank for that.

00:22:43.149 --> 00:22:48.009
That then takes us to the final item on the
agenda, which is the reports from Board Committees,

00:22:48.009 --> 00:22:54.379
as I indicated at the outset. We will take
two of these as style[?] items, not for discussion.

00:22:54.379 --> 00:22:59.299
The Board will remember that at our last meeting,
we agreed upon a reorganisation of some of

00:22:59.299 --> 00:23:05.279
the committees. So that we not have a Strategic
Commissioning and Assurance Committee and

00:23:05.279 --> 00:23:12.279
a new Specialised Commissioning Committee.
The inaugural meeting of the Strategic Commissioning

00:23:13.799 --> 00:23:18.340
Committee took place yesterday. I am going
to invite David to give us a brief summary

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:19.369
of what transpired.

00:23:19.369 --> 00:23:24.470
David Roberts: Thank you Malcolm, as you say,
the Committee met for the first time yesterday.

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:31.169
It was a kick off meeting and brainstorming
meeting to agree priorities and ways of working.

00:23:31.169 --> 00:23:36.749
Out of that, it is clear that our overall
goal for the Commissioning Committee needs

00:23:36.749 --> 00:23:42.419
to be to ensure that the commissioning system
and its processes are delivering demonstrable

00:23:42.419 --> 00:23:49.419
value for the investments that are made. The
specific activities that we will review include

00:23:54.049 --> 00:23:59.869
items such as how the commissioning processes
are going to support the implementation of

00:23:59.869 --> 00:24:05.659
new models of care, initially in the vanguard
sites, as well as supporting the works to

00:24:05.659 --> 00:24:11.950
transform the care models for key priorities
within our business plan, such as cancer,

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:13.330
mental health and learning disability.

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:19.450
In addition, there are clearly a range of
ongoing items which will require Board and

00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:24.769
Board Committee signoff, as well as I think
really importantly capturing the outcomes

00:24:24.769 --> 00:24:31.649
and, as importantly, the learnings from the
commissioning assurance activities, both for

00:24:31.649 --> 00:24:37.889
ourselves and in ways that we can inform the
commissioning system. That too will also form

00:24:37.889 --> 00:24:45.929
a major part of our agenda. In thinking about
the decisions that we will take, it is clear

00:24:45.999 --> 00:24:52.190
that the delivery of quality and the views
and impacts of patients, as well as efficiency

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:57.649
and absolute financial performance, will guide
us in thinking through the appropriate balance

00:24:57.649 --> 00:25:00.889
in our decision making.

00:25:00.889 --> 00:25:06.799
Finally the Commissioning Committee reviewed,
in some depth, the approach for ensuring that

00:25:06.799 --> 00:25:13.499
quality is present in all conversations regarding
commissioning. In particular, we agreed the

00:25:13.499 --> 00:25:17.429
governance arrangements, including the remit
of the commissioning committee relative to

00:25:17.429 --> 00:25:23.700
the full board and to the National Quality
Board, and how that will be reported in. The

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:30.090
next step is for management to consider the
priorities that were set, alongside the list

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:35.979
of decisions where committee involvement is
necessary, and then to propose a work plan

00:25:35.979 --> 00:25:39.369
and rolling agenda for the commissioning Committee.

00:25:39.369 --> 00:25:45.080
So in summary, it was a good start, a limbering
up, if you like, for the real work that lies

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:51.340
ahead of us and I look forward to reporting
in due course, at further Board meetings on

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:55.929
the substance of the issues that we are grappling
with.

00:25:55.929 --> 00:26:01.149
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for that
David. May I invite comments and questions

00:26:01.149 --> 00:26:06.269
from members of the Board? Some of us, of
course, were at the meeting. No? Well, thank

00:26:06.269 --> 00:26:07.299
you very much.

00:26:07.299 --> 00:26:13.119
I think that the only thing I would add is
the sheer importance of this committee grappling

00:26:13.119 --> 00:26:17.940
up with how we adapt the whole commission
process around the Forward View and the new

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:23.950
models of care and how we use commissioning
in such a way as to bring about or to facilitate

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:28.429
the changes that we know we need to bring
about across the whole of the provision in

00:26:28.429 --> 00:26:30.599
the NHS in England.

00:26:30.599 --> 00:26:34.960
Right, well, thank you very much. That brings
us to the end of the formal business. I have

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:40.450
had no indication of any other business and
I have none myself, except to report that

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:47.450
the next meeting of the Board will be on Thursday,
26th March in Leeds. We go now into private

00:26:48.429 --> 00:26:52.239
session and I declare the public section of
the Board meeting to be closed. Thank you

00:26:52.239 --> 00:26:52.610
very much.

